<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Sedan</h1><p class="st-meta">1424 CE – 1642 CE (approx.) · capital Sedan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Protestant sovereign principality on the Meuse, ruled by the La Marck and La Tour d'Auvergne families, which sheltered Huguenots and French rebels.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1424 Everard de la Marck acquired the lordship of Sedan on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a>, at the frontier between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/prince-bishopric-of-liege">Liège</a>. His descendants built a great fortress there and claimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, owing allegiance to neither the French king nor the emperor.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-ii-de-la-marck">Robert II de la Marck</a> and his successors were also dukes of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-bouillon">Bouillon</a>. The princes adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calvinism">Calvinism</a>, and Sedan became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenot</a> refuge with a Protestant academy founded in 1602. In 1591 the heiress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlotte-de-la-marck">Charlotte de la Marck</a> married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-de-la-tour-dauvergne-duke-of-bouillon">Henri de La Tour d'Auvergne</a>, father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-de-la-tour-d-auvergne-viscount-of-turenne">Marshal Turenne</a>, who was born at Sedan in 1611. Sedan's cloth industry later flourished.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederic-maurice-de-la-tour-dauvergne">Frédéric-Maurice de La Tour d'Auvergne</a> joined the conspiracy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-coiffier-de-ruze-marquis-of-cinq-mars">Cinq-Mars</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cardinal-richelieu">Richelieu</a>, and to save his life ceded Sedan to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiii-of-france">Louis XIII</a> in 1642. The principality was absorbed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-sedan.jpg" alt="Principality of Sedan" width="320" height="221"><figcaption>Image: Garitan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20_la_principaut%C3%A9_de_Sedan_Davanne-Chappier.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1424 CE – 1642 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sedan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Robert II de la Marck, Henri de La Tour d'Auvergne, Duke of Bouillon, Frédéric-Maurice de La Tour d'Auvergne</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Calvinism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sedan, Sovereignty of Sedan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1533&amp;focus=principality-of-sedan" aria-label="Principality of Sedan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4793 447 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Sedan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5113 703L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5113 703h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5113 703h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Sedan. <a href="/atlas?year=1533&amp;focus=principality-of-sedan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

